* START-HISTORY:
* 12 Sep 26 dm  written, from the Windows port's SET_ACC_PASSWORD of 15-20 Aug
*               2026.  PORT_ADOPTION 17.  The port's grammar and refusals; the
*               credential half is replaced by passwd(1), because there is no
*               $cred register on Linux and there should not be one.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    MODIFY.PASSWORD [account]
*
*    Sets the password of an SD account.  With no account name, sets your own.
*
*    THE PASSWORD IS NEVER AN ARGUMENT.  It is read by passwd(1) from its own
*    prompts, and anything after the account name is refused rather than
*    ignored - see the comment on that test below, which is the port's and is
*    worth every line of it.
*
*    Setting someone else's requires administrator rights.  Setting your own
*    requires your current password, and SD does not ask for it or check it:
*    passwd(1) does, through PAM.
*
* ***THE WHOLE CREDENTIAL HALF OF THE PORT'S VERSION IS GONE, AND THAT IS THE
* POINT OF THE LINUX VERSION RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.***  The port keeps its own
* $cred register - a SCRAM stored key per account - because a Windows SD account
* is not a Windows logon.  ***HERE AN SD ACCOUNT IS A LINUX USER***: sd is not
* setuid, the session runs as that user, and LOGIN's gate is Linux group
* membership.  So the account's password IS the Linux password, and inventing a
* second one would create a credential that could disagree with the real one.
*
* ***SD THEREFORE NEVER SEES A PASSWORD.***  It does not prompt for one, hold
* one in a variable, or pass one to anything.  Both arms below hand off to
* passwd(1) and let it do its own prompting on the terminal.  That is a
* stronger position than the port can reach on Windows, and it is why there is
* no $cred, no !CRED_SET and no !CRED_VERIFY in this tree.
*
* ***THE TWO ARMS ARE SPLIT ON kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR), NOT ON "own", AND THE
* DIFFERENCE MATTERS.***
*
*    - An administrator session (which on Linux means "sudo sd", since
*      CPROC:328 grants the flag only when the real uid is 0) goes through
*      !set_passwd, i.e. "sudo sd-elevate passwd <user>", which runs passwd as
*      root and does NOT ask for the current one.  That is the port's rule -
*      an administrator resetting a forgotten password does not know it - and
*      it is also simply true of the machine: the session is already root.
*
*    - Any other session may only reach its OWN account, and runs
*      "passwd -- <user>" as itself.  PAM then demands the current password.
*      ***SO "you must know your current password" IS ENFORCED BY PAM AND NOT
*      BY SD***, which is the right place for it: no SD code can be wrong about
*      it, and the rule follows the machine's own password policy for free.
*
* ***THE USER NAME IS ALWAYS PASSED EXPLICITLY, NEVER OMITTED.***  A bare
* "passwd" changes the password of whoever is running it - and under "sudo sd"
* that is ROOT.  Naming the target every time is what stops this verb ever
* being pointed at the root account by accident.
*
*    An account with no Linux user of its own - a GROUP or OTHER account, whose
*    ACC$GROUP is not sdu_<name> - is refused (10913).  There is no password to
*    set: such an account is entered by whoever its group admits.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program set_acc_password
$catalog $MODIFY.PASSWORD

$include parser.h
$include int$keys.h
$include err.h
$include keys.h

deffun set_passwd(uname) calling '!set_passwd'
deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

   prompt ''
   parser = "!PARSER"
   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS

   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)   ;* verb

   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)
   if token.type = PARSER$END then
      account = upcase(@logname)
      own = @true
   end else
      account = upcase(token)
      own = (account = upcase(@logname))

* 12 Sep 26 dm - A TRAILING TOKEN IS REFUSED, NOT DISCARDED.  The port's test
*   and the port's reasoning, 20 Aug 2026, found by its owner typing it.
*   "MODIFY.PASSWORD DON hunter2" would otherwise set account = DON, throw
*   "hunter2" away without a word, and prompt as though nothing had happened -
*   every visible sign saying it worked, because it did work, just not the way
*   the person believed.
*
*   ***THE COST OF SAYING NOTHING IS THAT THE PASSWORD IS ALREADY SOMEWHERE
*   ELSE by the time the prompt appears***: in SD's command stack, and in a
*   shell's history and process list if the verb is ever reached from one.
*   Refusing does not put it back - it tells the person to go and deal with it
*   instead of leaving them believing the command-line form is supported.
*
*   IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT "ACCEPT IT IF GIVEN".  A password on a command line
*   is the thing this verb exists to avoid.
      call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)
      if token.type # PARSER$END then
         stop sysmsg(5276)  ;* A password is never given on the command line
      end
   end

   * Only an administrator may set an account other than their own.

   if not(own) and not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end

   * The account has to be a real one.

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'ACCOUNTS' to acc.f else
      stop sysmsg(2200)  ;* Cannot open accounts register
   end

   read acc.rec from acc.f, account else
      close acc.f
      stop sysmsg(5018, account)  ;* Account %1 not in register
   end
   close acc.f

*  THE LINUX USER IS READ FROM THE REGISTER, NOT GUESSED FROM THE ACCOUNT NAME.
*  CREATE.ACCOUNT writes ACC$GROUP as sdu_<user>, and MODIFYA's set.tier and
*  os.set both find the person the same way; an account whose group is not
*  sdu_ has no single person behind it.
   pw.grp = trim(acc.rec<ACC$GROUP>)
   pw.user = ''
   if pw.grp[1,4] = 'sdu_' then pw.user = pw.grp[5,999]

   if pw.user = '' or not(valid_os_name(pw.user)) then
      @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
      stop sysmsg(10913, account)  ;* %1 has no Linux user of its own
   end

   if kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) then
*     Root sets it.  No current password is asked for, and none is known.
      if set_passwd(pw.user) then
         @system.return.code = 0
         crt sysmsg(10914, account)
      end else
         @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
         stop sysmsg(10915, account, status())
      end
   end else
*     As the person themselves, so PAM demands the current password.  The name
*     is explicit: a bare "passwd" would target whoever is running it.
      os.execute 'passwd -- ' : pw.user
      pw.rc = OS.ERROR()
      if pw.rc = 0 then
         @system.return.code = 0
         crt sysmsg(10914, account)
      end else
         @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
         stop sysmsg(10915, account, pw.rc)
      end
   end

   stop

end

* END-CODE
